WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.080
Was Marilyn Monroe's real life not&nbsp;
interesting enough? Why else would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.900
the makers of "Blonde" invent&nbsp;
details about her real father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.200
a polyamorous triad, and her&nbsp;
affair with a president?

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:16.380
The film attributes much of Marilyn Monroe's inner&nbsp;
torment, professional ambition, and dysfunctional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.540
relationships to an obsessive search for the&nbsp;
father she never knew. Early on, her mother Gladys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:27.000
shows a young Norma Jeane Mortenson a framed&nbsp;
photograph of a dashing older man. This man is her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.620
father, Gladys tells her, a powerful man in the&nbsp;
movie business but whose name she can never tell.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.820
"And one day I will return&nbsp;
to Los Angeles to claim you."

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.920
Monroe will spend the rest of her life&nbsp;
seeking him out. At the height of her stardom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.000
she begins to receive letters from a man&nbsp;
claiming to be her father, and the cruel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.800
revelation of the letters' true author is the&nbsp;
push that sets Monroe toward taking her own life.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.720
While it's true that Monroe was raised without&nbsp;
a father in real life, she strongly suspected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.980
her father was a man named Charles Stanley&nbsp;
Gifford, whom Gladys had an affair with while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.560
working as a film editor at RKO Pictures. In&nbsp;
1952, Monroe even attempted to contact him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.720
according to biographer Charles Casillo, and the&nbsp;
anonymous letters signed "Tearful Father" are an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:16.140
invention of the novel and film. In 2022, a French&nbsp;
DNA researcher apparently proved that Gifford and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.120
Monroe were related; his findings were broadcast&nbsp;
in the documentary Marilyn, Her Final Secret.
 &nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.340
Marilyn Monroe's fraught upbringing with&nbsp;
her mother Gladys is represented primarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.960
by two hellish, frightening sequences early&nbsp;
in the film. The first is when Gladys puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.120
young Norma Jeane in their car and attempts to&nbsp;
drive into the Griffith Park wildfire of 1933;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:41.040
the second is when she tries to drown her daughter&nbsp;
in the bathtub. After the bathtub incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.620
Gladys is hospitalized and confined to a mental&nbsp;
institution for the rest of her life. Norma Jeane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.820
after being cared for by her neighbors for a&nbsp;
short time, is dropped off at an orphanage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.960
When she sees her mother again, 10 years&nbsp;
later, Norma Jeane has transformed fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.460
into Marilyn, and Gladys — in the throes&nbsp;
of schizophrenia — doesn't recognize her.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.140
While the specific incidents presented here may&nbsp;
not be confirmed, both Gladys and her mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.880
Della Monroe, were known to have violent&nbsp;
outbursts related to mental illness. Monroe's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.320
earliest years were mostly spent in the care&nbsp;
of family friends. In an incident detailed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:18.420
J. Randy Taraborrelli's book The Secret Life&nbsp;
of Marilyn Monroe, Della talked her way into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.980
the home where infant Norma Jeane was living&nbsp;
and tried to smother her with a pillow. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.240
when Norma Jeane was three, Gladys attempted&nbsp;
to abduct her by stuffing her in a duffel bag.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:33.000
After Gladys was institutionalized in 1934,&nbsp;
Norma Jeane was a ward of the state, living in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.280
orphanages and foster homes before being taken&nbsp;
in by family friend Grace Goddard at age 11.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.400
In Blonde, Marilyn Monroe's big break comes after&nbsp;
years spent as a pin-up model, when she's offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:50.640
an audition for a studio mogul referred to as Mr.&nbsp;
Z, played by David Warshofsky. Stepping into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.620
intimidating office full of taxidermied animals,&nbsp;
Monroe nervously reads her script in front of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.660
silent Mr. Z until the man steps behind her,&nbsp;
still without a word, and sexually assaults&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.040
her. Later in the film, when she's talking about&nbsp;
the beginning of her career with the ex-athlete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.020
the film's stand-in for second husband&nbsp;
Joe DiMaggio, played by Bobby Cannavale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.700
Marilyn flashes back to this moment and simply&nbsp;
tells him that she was, quote, "discovered."

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.740
Mr. Z is by all accounts an avatar for&nbsp;
20th Century Fox head Darryl F. Zanuck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.060
Monroe was under contract at Fox for much&nbsp;
of her film career, and she and Zanuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.380
are known to have had a difficult working&nbsp;
relationship. Monroe bristled at the "dumb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:33.540
blonde" role she was regularly offered, while&nbsp;
he considered her simply that — a dumb blonde.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.860
And while tales of the "casting couch"&nbsp;
were common in Hollywood both then and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.780
according to biographer Anthony Summers, there's&nbsp;
no evidence that Zanuck assaulted Monroe in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.380
manner. As to her being "discovered,"&nbsp;
Fox's official story was that she'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:50.400
been discovered while babysitting for a casting&nbsp;
director, initially hiding the fact that she'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.020
been a pin-up and occasional nude model for&nbsp;
years before being cast in her first film.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:02.040
In Blonde, Marilyn Monroe is seen in 1951&nbsp;
auditioning for the Richard Widmark thriller Don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.900
Bother to Knock. In a tight close-up mimicking&nbsp;
the angle of the screen test's camera, Monroe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.640
gives an intense, soul-baring monologue. The&nbsp;
role is uncomfortably close to her own life — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.080
woman whose mother was abusive and whose father&nbsp;
had abandoned her long ago. After she's done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.640
she pleads for another chance but is taken&nbsp;
away by her manager. The crew is dumbfounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.140
by what they saw as a terrible performance,&nbsp;
but the director is captivated by her looks.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.040
The scene treats Monroe as if she's an&nbsp;
unknown at this time, as if she's never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.240
stepped in front of the camera. In reality,&nbsp;
she was a sensation almost from the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.860
she appeared on film. In fact, the year before,&nbsp;
she'd made two of her most notable early movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:43.500
All About Eve and John Huston's noir The Asphalt&nbsp;
Jungle. She'd even weathered a scandal involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:48.600
nude photos she'd posed for in 1949. As for Don't&nbsp;
Bother to Knock, the film received mixed reviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.080
but Monroe's performance was singled&nbsp;
out for praise, with Variety writing,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.940
"Marilyn Monroe ... gives an excellent account&nbsp;
of herself in a strictly dramatic role."

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:01.080
Marilyn Monroe was romantically&nbsp;
linked to any number of Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:05.220
luminaries in the early 1950s before&nbsp;
her relationship with Joe DiMaggio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.780
including Marlon Brando and director Elia&nbsp;
Kazan. The film represents this time in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:13.800
life by focusing on an unconventional&nbsp;
romance with two show business scions:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:20.340
Charlie Chaplin Jr., nicknamed Cass, and Edward&nbsp;
G. Robinson Jr., known as Eddy G. Monroe envies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.960
them because everyone in the world knows who&nbsp;
their fathers are, while Cass and Eddy G. envy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.500
her for the fact that she's an orphan and&nbsp;
can make herself into anything she wishes.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.160
"We're juniors. With men who never wanted us."

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.220
The three do everything together, and when Monroe&nbsp;
gets pregnant, they celebrate as a group. Monroe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.900
was involved with Chaplin in real life for a short&nbsp;
time, according to biographer Anthony Summers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.680
but the relationship apparently came to an&nbsp;
end after she had an affair with Chaplin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.060
brother. Robinson isn't known to have dated&nbsp;
Monroe or Chaplin, either separately or in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:56.700
the polyamorous triad the film depicts. The film&nbsp;
also has Chaplin dying before Monroe, and in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:00.900
posthumous communication to her, he reveals that&nbsp;
he was the author of all the letters her "father"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:07.080
wrote to her. In real life, Chaplin would outlive&nbsp;
Monroe by six years, dying in 1968 at age 42.
 &nbsp;

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:12.720
In the film, Marilyn Monroe balks at her&nbsp;
contracted $500 a week salary for appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.900
in Howard Hawks' musical Gentlemen&nbsp;
Prefer Blondes when she learns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:20.700
co-star Jane Russell is making $100,000.&nbsp;
After all, she's the blonde of the title.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:28.320
"And I'm playing the blonde in 'Gentlemen&nbsp;
prefer blondes'? That's an insult."

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.600
But there are other complications in taking&nbsp;
the role beyond money. Her pregnancy is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.020
professional barrier, and she also fears&nbsp;
that her mother's mental illness could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:40.920
be hereditary. At the studio's insistence,&nbsp;
Monroe decides to terminate the pregnancy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.580
leading to the first of the film's&nbsp;
two graphic abortion sequences.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:50.220
Verifying the accuracy of Monroe's abortions, or&nbsp;
even if she had any, is difficult. It was illegal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:55.560
nationwide to terminate a pregnancy in 1953, and&nbsp;
the notion that Monroe had several abortions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:01.260
the course of her life was popularized by author&nbsp;
Gloria Steinem in her 1986 biography Marilyn:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:06.000
Norma Jean. However, it is known that Monroe&nbsp;
was paid considerably less for her star-making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.560
role in Gentlemen Prefer Blondes. It was one&nbsp;
of many small insults during her time under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.660
the thumb of Darryl F. Zanuck and a large part of&nbsp;
her motivation to break her contract with Fox and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:20.040
start her own production company. This, however,&nbsp;
is a chapter in her life that the film ignores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.880
along with any other sense that Monroe&nbsp;
was an active participant in her career.
 &nbsp;

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:29.100
After Marilyn Monroe's abusive marriage&nbsp;
to the Ex-Athlete ends in divorce, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:33.240
retreats to New York City to study acting.&nbsp;
When the Playwright, the film's stand-in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.320
for Arthur Miller, played by Adrien Brody,&nbsp;
sees her in a reading of one of his plays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:41.220
he assumes that she's been cast because the&nbsp;
director is in love with her. But as the lights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:45.660
come up after the reading, the Playwright is&nbsp;
in tears at her performance. Monroe impresses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:50.160
him with how well-read she is, while she&nbsp;
connects to his gentle intellectualism — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.320
complete opposite of the prudish brutality&nbsp;
of the Ex-Athlete. The two marry in 1956.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:59.940
The film frames the play reading as the first&nbsp;
time they meet. The Playwright has heard of her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:04.140
obviously, but they don't know each other. In&nbsp;
real life, Miller and Monroe met in Los Angeles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:10.260
in 1950. The two would correspond off and on over&nbsp;
the years until reconnecting in New York in 1955&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:15.060
while Monroe was studying at the Actors Studio&nbsp;
and managing her own production company. Miller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.960
however, was married at the time of their&nbsp;
affair and under investigation by the House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.320
Un-American Activities Committee&nbsp;
for alleged communist affiliations.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:28.200
"There is nothing a politician won't stoop to if&nbsp;
it means some publicity for him or some power."

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.220
Monroe stood by him through both&nbsp;
his divorce and his legal troubles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.100
none of which is given any&nbsp;
space in Andrew Dominik's film.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.860
The affair between Marilyn Monroe and John F.&nbsp;
Kennedy has always been the stuff of second-hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:46.380
reports and urban legends rather than established&nbsp;
fact. According to Donald Spoto's comprehensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:51.900
1993 biography of Monroe, the two were only ever&nbsp;
seen in public together four times. Biographer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:56.160
Anthony Summers, on the other hand, treats it&nbsp;
very much as a fact that she was involved with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:01.140
both JFK and his brother, Robert, that her final&nbsp;
days were preoccupied with thoughts of both men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.340
and that JFK may have even visited her&nbsp;
in California shortly before her death.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:10.500
The film dramatizes her alleged relationship with&nbsp;
Kennedy via a nightmarish sequence in which an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.420
inebriated Monroe is escorted off an airplane by&nbsp;
Secret Service agents, literally dragged through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:19.920
the back hallways of a New York hotel. She's&nbsp;
then placed in the President's hotel room where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:24.180
she's essentially forced to do something against&nbsp;
her will. Afterward, she's escorted right back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:29.040
out by Secret Service. That nightmare feeling&nbsp;
continues in a later scene where she dreams of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:33.360
men kidnapping her in the middle of the night&nbsp;
and forcibly removing what we're to assume is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.340
her baby with the President, the second of the&nbsp;
film's explicit and disturbing abortion scenes.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:44.880
During Blonde, it's 1962, and Marilyn Monroe&nbsp;
receives a call from Eddy G. with the sad news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:50.160
that Cass has died. He informs her that Cass left&nbsp;
her something and to watch out for a delivery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.140
When that delivery comes, it's an old stuffed&nbsp;
tiger like the one she had as a girl, along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:59.820
a note containing a terrible, cruel confession:&nbsp;
Cass was the one writing letters to her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:05.280
claiming to be her father. The revelation sends&nbsp;
Monroe into a tailspin of anguish. Standing by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.020
pool of her Hollywood home, she swallows dozens&nbsp;
of pills, one after the other. She strips off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:15.420
her clothes and climbs into bed, takes the phone&nbsp;
off the hook, and passes out. As she slowly dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:20.040
she has a vision of the man she thought was her&nbsp;
father, his face beckoning her from the heavens.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:25.200
However, Monroe knew who her father was and&nbsp;
neither Cass nor anyone else wrote her letters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:29.040
like that. The entire sequence, other than&nbsp;
the condition Monroe's body was found in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.660
is a flight of fancy. So what might have&nbsp;
happened? The Los Angeles coroner at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:36.780
time ruled her death a "probable&nbsp;
suicide" due to the sheer amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:40.500
of barbiturates she'd ingested. Yet her&nbsp;
personal correspondence from that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:45.420
collected and published in 2010 as the book&nbsp;
Fragments, shows a side of her that finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:50.040
felt on firm ground, leading credence to&nbsp;
the idea that her death was accidental.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:53.340
If you or anyone you know has&nbsp;
been a victim of sexual assault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:56.700
help is available. Visit the Rape,&nbsp;
Abuse &amp; Incest National Network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:02.880
website or contact RAINN's National&nbsp;
Helpline at 1-800-656-HOPE (4673).

